<% content_for :stylesheets, area_stylesheet_link_tags("simulator") %>

<header class="page-hero page-hero--compact">
  <h1>Take the controls in the simulator</h1>
  <p class="page-hero-subtitle">Start with Go on its own. Find an aircraft on the radar. If it&rsquo;s at the top right, look ahead and to your right.</p>
</header>

<section id="simulator">
  <%= render "shared/simulator_loader" %>
</section>

<section id="exercise" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">Read a symbol and follow its line</h2>
  <p>The point tells you whether the aircraft is above or below you. If it&rsquo;s flying at roughly your level, you&rsquo;ll see a diamond. The line shows where the aircraft should be in a minute if it keeps flying the same track. When a sector flashes, look for traffic in that direction.</p>
  <p><%= link_to_page "radar", label: "See the radar symbols", anchor: "shapes" %></p>
</section>

<section id="compare" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Compare Go on its own with Tango</h2>
  <p>With Go on its own, you see nearby ADS-L traffic, which shares position by radio under the European standard, and FLARM traffic, which uses a system common in gliding.</p>
  <p>Tick &quot;With Bravo (skyBlip Tango)&quot;. The simulator restarts with a Go paired to a Bravo. Nearby traffic is still ADS-L and FLARM. Bravo adds aircraft that transmit their positions by radio using ADS-B. They start between 8 and 30 km away in this scenario.</p>
  <p>Look at the band around the radar circle. It shows the direction of traffic beyond the circle.</p>
  <p class="signature"><%= link_to_page "buy", label: "Choose my device", class: "button button--primary" %></p>
</section>
